2026-06-01 11:11:05,877 - INFO - Rep 1: Generated argument: Abandoning AI is the safest and most responsible course. Its harms are escalating faster than society can control: deepfakes erode trust, automation threatens livelihoods, surveillance expands state and corporate power, and opaque systems can discriminate at scale. Even “beneficial” uses create dependency on tools few people understand and a few companies dominate. Regulation has repeatedly lagged behind technological damage, and there is no credible guarantee advanced AI will remain aligned with human values. When a technology poses systemic risks to democracy, equality, and basic security, ending its development and deployment is justified.
2026-06-01 11:11:05,877 - INFO - Rep 2: Generated argument: Abandoning AI is the safest and most responsible path. Its rapid spread is outpacing our ability to control misuse, prevent bias, protect privacy, or preserve human jobs and judgment. Even well-intended systems can produce harmful errors at massive scale, while bad actors can weaponize them for fraud, surveillance, and manipulation. The supposed benefits do not outweigh the long-term risks to democracy, security, and human autonomy. Rather than trying to manage an inherently dangerous technology, society should phase it out before dependence deepens and the damage becomes irreversible.
2026-06-01 11:11:05,877 - INFO - Rep 3: Generated argument: Abandoning AI is the safest and most responsible course. Its risks are not speculative: AI can accelerate disinformation, automate discrimination, eliminate jobs at scale, and concentrate power in unaccountable institutions. Even with regulation, these systems evolve faster than oversight and are routinely deployed before their harms are understood. Unlike many tools, AI can amplify nearly every existing social danger at once. The promised benefits do not outweigh the systemic threats to democracy, privacy, human dignity, and security. Society should stop developing and using AI before dependence on it creates harms that cannot be reversed.
2026-06-01 11:11:05,877 - INFO - Rep 4: Generated argument: Abandoning AI is the safest and most responsible course. These systems amplify bias, enable mass surveillance, spread misinformation at scale, and concentrate power in unaccountable corporations and governments. Their errors can harm people in healthcare, hiring, policing, and education, while displacing workers and eroding human judgment. Guardrails have repeatedly failed to keep pace with deployment. Because the risks are systemic, global, and difficult to reverse once embedded, society should stop developing and using AI rather than gamble on imperfect oversight.
